    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with the specificity requirements for certain dermatological presentations in our recent audits. Specifically, when we see atypical morphology—like irregular, pigmented lesions that don't quite fit the standard melanocytic nevus criteria, or dermatological manifestations with unusual, granular, or uneven textures—the claims are being flagged for insufficient specificity. Can you clarify the hierarchy for selecting ICD-10 codes when the skin irregularity presents with these non-standard, irregular, or \"unusual\" structural characteristics?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When selecting ICD-10 codes for atypical dermatological presentations, you must prioritize the most specific code that captures the unique morphological characteristics described, rather than defaulting to the most general category. This involves carefully reviewing the index from the skin irregularity's specific descriptors (e.g., \"granular,\" \"irregular pigmentation\") to find the narrowest clinically accurate code available in Chapter VI. If no highly specific code exists for the precise unusual texture, selecting the most descriptive code within subcategories for that condition is still preferred over a broad, non-specific diagnosis code. Always ensure the included code fully reflects the complex presentation to satisfy audit requirements regarding diagnostic clarity.",
      "source": "target",
