    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. One thing our auditors are really hung up on is when the physician uses descriptive terminology that feels almost \"otherworldly\" or just highly aberrant—like lesions that appear \"grotesque,\" \"earthy,\" or have a \"gnarled\" texture. When the descriptors used in the clinical notes aren't standard medical terms and instead sound quite strange or even folklore-adjacent, how do we ensure the coder translates that \"bizarre\" or \"uncommon\" visual presentation into the correct ICD-10 hierarchy without losing the essence of that irregularity?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When clinical notes use non-standard or unusual terminology like \"grotesque\" or \"gnarled,\" you must rely on medical dictionaries or local descriptors to translate these phrases into their closest standard ICD-10 clinical findings or procedural terms. For instance, words like \"mottled,\" \"pitted,\" or \"igniting\" with specific textures often map to codes under Skin Lesions with Other Descriptive Adjectives in Chapter VI or their Explanatory Notes. Always verify the selected code's Official Descriptor on the Tabular List to ensure it captures the specific irregular morphology you are billing, as auditors are evaluating whether the code's official text matches the physician's detailed observation. If no exact match exists for such esoteric descriptions, selecting the most specific general category or interface with the Index is acceptable, provided the full clinical note supports the complexity of the diagnosis.",
      "source": "target",
